Why? Let me look: `flags()` computes binders via `split_binders`, which finds the *last* top-level arrow/comma. For type text:
`Poincare.D12.SemanticLedger.a3CtlTauto (P : Prop) (h : P) : P`
After `strip_name`: `(P : Prop) (h : P) : P`. The loop scans for top-level `→` or `,` — there's no arrow or comma at depth 0 (the colons are at depth 0 but not in the set). So `last == -1` → returns `[], s.strip()` → binders empty, conclusion = whole text `(P : Prop) (h : P) : P`. Then T9/T10 don't fire. So the screen only handles arrow/comma-separated binders, not `#check`-style colon-separated binder groups!

Wait — but the real probe logs: how did vacuity_screen3 parse them? The probe logs' `#check` output is in the format `name :\n  type` or `name (binders) : conclusion`? Looking at the earlier probe head:
```
Poincare.D12.SemanticLedger.not_initialCondition_gaussian :
  ¬Poincare.D12.SemanticLedger.D7InitialConditionAt ... 0
```
and
```
Poincare.D12.SemanticLedger.not_initialCondition_gaussian_quantified :
  ¬∀ (x : ℝ) (f : ℝ → ℝ), ...
```
So the type is on continuation lines; `parse_probe` joins them. The type string then is `¬∀ (x : ℝ) ..., ...` etc. For theorems with explicit binders before the colon (like `(P : Prop) (h : P) : P`), Lean pretty-prints... Let me check an actual probe entry with binders: search round-7 probe logs for a line matching `^Poincare.*\) : ` (i.e., binder group before colon). Example: `heatEvolve_norm_le`? Let me grep.

This matters: if real card theorems with explicit non-arrow binders exist, T9/T10 may be blind to them. The round-3 screen claimed zero flags for T9/T10 — consistent with the criteria being implemented but possibly ineffective on binder-style types.

